From e89d217a5373bb1d1e97890304f8ced49709aa69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_sched_tc_fixed <>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 19:27:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] apps/examples/testcase: add preemption control and priority
 inversion families

Phase 4 of the scenario test rollout.  These two families cover the parts of
the scheduler where a defect is silent: nothing returns an error, nothing
deadlocks, a deadline is simply missed.

The harness gains two timing helpers.  stc_busy_wait_ms spins without ever
blocking or yielding, which the lock scenarios require: any sleeping call
would hand the CPU away and satisfy the wake for reasons unrelated to the
lock, making the scenario pass regardless of what the lock did.

stc_sched_lock adds three scenarios.  LOCK-01 parks a high priority task on
a semaphore, takes the lock, posts, and proves across two hundred
milliseconds of busy waiting that the task did not run, then proves it does
once unlocked.  LOCK-02 nests to depth eight and unwinds to depth one, where
preemption must still be disabled; an implementation treating the lock as a
boolean rather than a count passes LOCK-01 and fails here.  LOCK-03 makes
the wake originate on a different CPU from the lock holder, which pins down
semantics that are currently undocumented, namely whether sched_lock is a
promise about the calling CPU or about the system.

The catalogue's SCN-LOCK-04, which exits a task while it still holds the
lock, is deliberately not implemented.  Driving that abnormal exit path can
trip a kernel assertion rather than fail cleanly, which would take the run
down rather than report a result.  A note in the source records why, so that
a reader comparing against the catalogue is not left wondering.

stc_sched_inversion adds four.  INV-01 builds the classic three task
inversion and measures the high priority task's wait, which separates the
inherited and uninherited outcomes by an order of magnitude and so tolerates
a generous bound.  INV-02 repeats it with the holder and the spinner on
different CPUs, a path a single core test cannot reach.  INV-03 chains two
mutexes so the boost has to propagate through an intermediate waiter, which
catches an implementation that boosts only the immediate holder; that
failure is invisible in the single mutex case.  INV-04 targets the pending
list inversion by comparing aggregate throughput with and without scheduler
lock churn.  It is explicitly a proxy, recorded as such in the source:
application code cannot observe the pending list, so the scenario infers the
inversion from its cost and uses a loose threshold intended to catch a
collapse rather than to measure overhead.

The inversion family depends on CONFIG_PRIORITY_INHERITANCE and skips
without it, since the inversion is then expected behaviour rather than a
defect.  INV-04 does not depend on it and always runs.  Both the SMP and
non-SMP compilation paths are warning free under -Wall -Wextra.
